1
00:00:02,446 --> 00:00:06,800
Okay, we next have five concepts to present to the council.

2
00:00:06,960 --> 00:00:13,600
Before we can publish a funding opportunity announcement, we have to present the concept in an open session.

3
00:00:13,760 --> 00:00:18,180
We always use the open session of council for concept approval.

4
00:00:18,766 --> 00:00:27,396
So we're going to begin with a concept titled Machine Learning and Artificial Intelligence Tools to Advance Genomic Translational Research.

5
00:00:27,920 --> 00:00:34,852
And Sandhya Zira Sagar, Program Director in the Office of Genomic Data Science, will present the concept.

6
00:00:38,062 --> 00:00:41,840
Well, thank you for this opportunity to present this concept.

7
00:00:42,000 --> 00:00:49,120
But before that, I'd like to thank Susan for that wonderful foundation that she has laid in order to present this.

8
00:00:49,200 --> 00:00:54,372
And I can see that we can definitely leverage a lot of resources that is being developed at the ODSS.

9
00:00:55,374 --> 00:01:03,236
So the concept is MLAI tools to advance genomic translational research, and we are presenting this for concept clearance.

10
00:01:04,080 --> 00:01:13,380
I'm presenting this on behalf of Ajay Pillai in the Division of Genomic Science and Rob Rowley in the Division of Genomic Medicine, who have co-led this effort.

11
00:01:14,702 --> 00:01:16,480
I'll start with a brief outline.

12
00:01:16,560 --> 00:01:33,508
I'll start with the objective and present the background and rationale for this objective and the scope, the budget, acknowledge the help that all my wonderful colleagues have given us, the feedback that they have given us, and then appreciate your feedback.

13
00:01:35,278 --> 00:01:47,076
The objective of this is to spur the development of novel machine learning and artificial intelligence tools to explore their potential to advance genomic translational research.

14
00:01:47,758 --> 00:02:00,996
Specifically, the aim is to model pleiotropy and variable penetrance through the learning and classification capabilities of MLAI to uncover novel relationships between genotypes and phenotypes.

15
00:02:01,742 --> 00:02:12,068
Tools will be developed in a shared agreed-upon ethical, legal, and social implications framework, and I think you see a lot of resonance with the previous talk on this.

16
00:02:14,800 --> 00:02:19,332
I'll start with the quick definitions, which I know you're all very familiar with.

17
00:02:20,206 --> 00:02:29,316
But we see artificial intelligence as the capability of a computer system to mimic human cognitive functions such as learning and problem solving.

18
00:02:30,382 --> 00:02:40,836
And machine learning is an application of AI whereby mathematical models of data are used to help a computer learn without direct instruction.

19
00:02:41,440 --> 00:02:47,428
Now, machine learning has been used for several decades for classification and for pattern recognition.

20
00:02:47,920 --> 00:02:59,172
However, with recent advances in computation resources such as those that Susan mentioned, they're increasingly able to leverage multimodal and large data sets.

21
00:03:00,398 --> 00:03:06,340
And in fact, MLAI is being used in biomedical research and in medicine.

22
00:03:07,214 --> 00:03:19,076
An example is one where MLAI approaches have been able to discover novel patterns from retinal imaging scans to help detect Alzheimer's disease.

23
00:03:19,840 --> 00:03:33,700
Now, the adaptive capability of these tools to use new data as they become available is particularly promising for their potential use to assist in clinical decision making.

24
00:03:35,822 --> 00:03:53,860
An example of that is where MLAI tools trained on detecting high potassium levels from electrocardiogram scan data are being used to non-invasively monitor these potassium levels.

25
00:03:53,920 --> 00:04:00,548
in, for example, chronic kidney disease patients in the Mayo Clinic in the clinical site setting.

26
00:04:01,134 --> 00:04:10,756
What this means is that MLAI tools developed in a research setting are being able to be translated gradually into medicine.

27
00:04:12,462 --> 00:04:29,344
We, however, see that we need to invest more in understanding the applicability and feasibility, and importantly, the LC to understand that in of these of these MLA approaches in genomic translational research.

28
00:04:29,504 --> 00:04:34,064
And that is the opportunity being presented in this concept.

29
00:04:35,246 --> 00:04:37,824
So let's take what an example may look like, right?

30
00:04:37,904 --> 00:04:49,252
We know that pathogenic variants, even those that are highly studied, due to variant penetrance and pleiotropy, result in phenotypic differences that remain unexplained.

31
00:04:49,504 --> 00:04:52,544
Let's take the example of cystic fibrosis, right?

32
00:04:53,104 --> 00:04:59,184
We know that more than 2,000 variants, for example, have been discovered in CFTR gene.

33
00:04:59,950 --> 00:05:04,260
And these result in varying phenotypic differences that are unexplained.

34
00:05:04,910 --> 00:05:11,904
And so you can see that in this table here, the pluses, you can see that varying number of pluses are here.

35
00:05:12,270 --> 00:05:18,596
For example, with the gene modifiers, with non-CFTR gene modifiers, as well as the environment.

36
00:05:19,502 --> 00:05:33,988
In fact, just last month, a paper showed that levels of mercury and molybdenum in the groundwater can affect the susceptibility of cystic fibrosis patients to certain mycobacterial strains.

37
00:05:34,544 --> 00:05:38,884
And this, as you can imagine, is a social determinants of health factor.

38
00:05:40,334 --> 00:05:55,652
If you look at interesting gene environment interactions, the ability of secondhand smoke to affect cystic fibrosis is modulated by variants in the transforming growth factor beta1 gene.

39
00:05:56,464 --> 00:06:08,868
We also know that therapeutic responsiveness to drugs that can actually help with symptoms in cystic fibrosis there is not very well understood.

40
00:06:09,358 --> 00:06:30,628
Wouldn't it be great if we could use these MLAI approaches to complement these existing efforts to understand penetrance and pleiotropy, to help understand the variability, so that we may be able to better understand therapeutic responsiveness or sensitivity to different factors?

41
00:06:33,038 --> 00:06:37,744
Feedback from recent NHGRI workshops, for example, the

42
00:06:41,264 --> 00:06:54,660
genomic medicine workshop in 2021, or the more focused machine learning and genomics workshop, have emphasized the need to invest in research to better translate findings from genomics.

43
00:06:55,184 --> 00:06:57,700
research into genomics, medicine.

44
00:06:58,606 --> 00:07:04,260
This has also been emphasized in the strategic vision document in 2020.

45
00:07:04,864 --> 00:07:19,620
And importantly, the vision also emphasized the need to understand the relationship between genomic and non-genomic computer contributions and such as the social and environmental contributions to health.

46
00:07:20,974 --> 00:07:33,060
So just a quick recap, then to highlight, the objective is to spur MLAI tool development to uncover novel relationships between genotypes.

47
00:07:33,326 --> 00:07:41,476
I keep bringing my cursor back here, between genotypes and phenotypes and in an agreed-upon LC framework.

48
00:07:43,584 --> 00:07:56,612
So this is an illustration from the strategic vision document describing the continuum between basic genomics research and clinical care.

49
00:07:57,264 --> 00:08:03,012
We see this initiative as a bridge between these two settings.

50
00:08:03,886 --> 00:08:12,388
The tools will be developed and validated in a translational research setting by clinical research physicians.

51
00:08:13,358 --> 00:08:25,476
And we hope and we think that the successful tools will then in the long term, the long term vision for these tools is for them to be tested in implementation settings.

52
00:08:26,144 --> 00:08:34,020
Which means that for this initiative, we do not intend to focus on adoption or implementation science.

53
00:08:36,464 --> 00:08:53,444
Coming to the scope, significant investments have been made to generate data sets, and we've just described, they've just been described genomics, omics, phenotypic data, variables, internet of things, sensor data.

54
00:08:54,350 --> 00:09:00,864
Along with these investments, we have also invested in interoperability standards, once again, that was discussed just now.

55
00:09:01,582 --> 00:09:15,844
Plus, with advances in computational resources and with data sharing policies, again described before, these have all helped to make or make helpful in making fair data more accessible.

56
00:09:16,544 --> 00:09:20,144
Very importantly, we have generated a lot of reference knowledge.

57
00:09:20,270 --> 00:09:23,664
So, for example, ClinGen that Eric in the morning mentioned.

58
00:09:24,078 --> 00:09:38,144
So these are all things that we think, we hope, can be used to train MLAI algorithms such that maybe we can help answer some of these questions.

59
00:09:38,624 --> 00:09:51,556
For example, in the case of CFTR, will we then be able to stratify these patients based on sensitivity to drugs or for susceptibility to infections?

60
00:09:52,782 --> 00:10:08,580
Now, for this initiative, we plan to focus on pleiotropy and penetrance specifically, and also to genes with known pathogenic variants for which there is a lot of data that's available.

61
00:10:09,134 --> 00:10:11,620
And you know, for example, the ACMG gene list.

62
00:10:11,984 --> 00:10:20,580
That's by no means the only list, but we intend to focus on genes that have a lot of data, diseases that have a lot of data in it.

63
00:10:21,744 --> 00:10:26,180
And this is, of course, something that we just brought about before.

64
00:10:26,704 --> 00:10:30,144
We plan to make sure that we try to address the LC concerns.

65
00:10:30,224 --> 00:10:40,624
And I won't go over the LC concerns because Susan just went over them, bias in data sets resulting in incorrect diagnosis or risk of compromising patient confidentiality.

66
00:10:40,944 --> 00:10:46,628
These are all concerns, and we intend to try to address them during the development of these tools.

67
00:10:47,118 --> 00:10:57,316
Therefore, we plan to ensure that the tools are developed and validated within a consortium established LC framework.

68
00:10:57,518 --> 00:11:05,796
So this is a collaborative project that we believe will be informed from contributions from several researchers.

69
00:11:08,174 --> 00:11:15,744
And I don't want to, I know she just talked about trustworthiness and interpretability, explainability, usability.

70
00:11:15,824 --> 00:11:19,652
These are all concepts within the LC that we will plan to address.

71
00:11:20,974 --> 00:11:23,364
So this is an explorative project.

72
00:11:24,704 --> 00:11:33,104
And you know, we want to make sure, we want to try to set ourselves up for a more flexible as well as an informed execution.

73
00:11:33,264 --> 00:11:38,784
And therefore, we plan to have a phased milestone-driven approach.

74
00:11:39,104 --> 00:11:47,904
And this was also thanks to the genomic data science working group with whom we had a pre-concept discussion, and that working group is actually a working group of this council.

75
00:11:48,224 --> 00:11:53,636
We had a discussion with them and they all recommended this biphasic structure.

76
00:11:54,224 --> 00:12:12,356
So we plan to have a design phase during which the consortium of researchers that we fund, the tool development sites, will select diseases and pathogenic variants depending on the data adequacy of the data to be representative of populations that are affected by the disease.

77
00:12:13,006 --> 00:12:16,784
Design tool endpoints and outputs and validation plan.

78
00:12:17,710 --> 00:12:25,424
Generalizability is an important feature that has been brought up before today about how NHGRI wants to have generalizable tools.

79
00:12:25,582 --> 00:12:34,276
And so one of the approaches we are going to use is make sure that the data sets are prepared for cross validation, that the tools can be cross validated.

80
00:12:35,168 --> 00:12:40,228
And as I mentioned, formulate draft best practices and LCOM framework.

81
00:12:40,608 --> 00:12:44,228
And this will be in phase in years one and two.

82
00:12:44,878 --> 00:12:51,140
At the end of the design phase, we will evaluate whether the objectives have been achieved.

83
00:12:51,502 --> 00:12:58,276
And if the objectives have been achieved, then we propose to go to the tool development phase.

84
00:12:58,766 --> 00:13:03,300
Here each site will develop tools according to the framework.

85
00:13:03,950 --> 00:13:06,368
They will validate according to this plan.

86
00:13:07,118 --> 00:13:11,812
Of course, there will need to be refining of these best practices and framework and the plans.

87
00:13:12,462 --> 00:13:18,600
They will disseminate resources fairly, and as you can imagine, tools, data sets, the framework, lessons, learned.

88
00:13:19,088 --> 00:13:21,476
And this will span years three to five.

89
00:13:21,808 --> 00:13:25,348
Now you can see that there is a lot of coordination that's involved.

90
00:13:25,582 --> 00:13:32,608
Therefore, we propose a coordination center to coordinate the logistics for all of these collaborative activities.

91
00:13:32,848 --> 00:13:40,612
And years three to five of this coordination center will, of course, be contingent on continuation of the development sites.

92
00:13:41,838 --> 00:13:58,660
Coming to the mechanisms, we propose two RFAs, one to fund the development sites and the other the coordination center, a UG3 for the design phase, UH3 for the tool development phase, and a UO1 to fund the coordination center.

93
00:14:00,910 --> 00:14:02,948
Relationship to ongoing activities.

94
00:14:03,888 --> 00:14:15,972
This is NHGRI's really first initiative to lay the foundation both methodologically and technically to develop these MLAI tools in an LC framework.

95
00:14:16,368 --> 00:14:30,084
And we definitely, however, plan to benefit by using the data sets that have been generated from any GRI programs as well as from the other NIH and global resources.

96
00:14:31,278 --> 00:14:33,568
Best practices and LC relevant resources.

97
00:14:33,648 --> 00:14:37,476
There are a lot that are generated, and we wish to leverage them.

98
00:14:37,678 --> 00:14:46,212
And I mentioned here just a couple of more recent ones that are being put forth.

99
00:14:48,528 --> 00:14:58,208
Coming to the budget, total annual costs of three to four sites for a total cost of 4.8 million, 1.2 million for the coordination center.

100
00:14:58,448 --> 00:15:01,060
Total cost for five years of 30 million.

101
00:15:01,408 --> 00:15:15,172
Now this is based on a multidisciplinary team with integrated expertise in MLAI, data wrangling, software development, clinical research, LCE, and coordination.

102
00:15:16,174 --> 00:15:20,196
Co-funding will be sought from other ICOs for additional sites.

